WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.710
How high can you count on one hand?

2
00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.660
Most people say five.

3
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.540
I can count to thirty-one.

4
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.740
By the end of this video, so can you.

5
00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:13.830
Today: how machines count

6
00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.930
with only ones and zeros.

7
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.380
Each switch inside a device is on or

8
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.660
off, one or zero: that's a bit.

9
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.720
One bit can say only two things:

10
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.080
yes or no, light or dark.

11
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.000
To say more, you need more bits.

12
00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.390
So each bit gets a value.

13
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.800
The bit on the right is worth one,

14
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.320
the next two, then four, eight and

15
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.320
sixteen: each double the last.

16
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:46.650
To read a number, add up

17
00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:47.730
the bits that are on.

18
00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.110
Let's read one zero one one zero.

19
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.880
Sixteen is on, eight is off, four

20
00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.820
and two are on, one is off.

21
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:05.300
Sixteen plus four plus two: twenty-two.

22
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.430
Now your hand.

23
00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:12.210
Your thumb is worth one, then two, four,

24
00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.450
eight, and your little finger sixteen.

25
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.640
A finger up is one; down is zero.

26
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.760
All five up makes thirty-one!

27
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:24.670
Quick check!

28
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:31.960
Here are five bits: one one zero zero one.

29
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.260
What number is that?

30
00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.290
Twenty-five: sixteen plus eight plus one.

31
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.010
If you said nineteen, you read

32
00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:42.950
it from the wrong end.

33
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.250
The biggest value is always on the left.

34
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.220
Eight bits make a byte.

35
00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.470
A byte counts from zero to two

36
00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.650
hundred and fifty-five: two hundred

37
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.070
and fifty-six values, enough for

38
00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.950
every English letter and digit.

39
00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.430
Everything a device stores is bytes.

40
00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:04.990
A temperature reading takes a few.

41
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.090
An English letter takes one,

42
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.070
an Arabic letter two.

43
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.470
A text message takes about a hundred, and

44
00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.630
a phone photo about three million.

45
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.410
More bits also mean more detail.

46
00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.010
A light sensor read with three

47
00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.190
bits has just eight levels.

48
00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.450
With ten bits, it has one thousand and

49
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:30.490
twenty-four, so it notices tiny changes.

50
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.900
But every extra bit costs more memory,

51
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.520
more data to send, and more battery.

52
00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.760
A huge photo looks great, but

53
00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.340
it eats your data bundle.

54
00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.350
So engineers choose just

55
00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:46.610
enough bits for the job.

56
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:48.370
Your turn.

57
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.960
A small sensor sends each

58
00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.300
reading with four bits.

59
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:54.840
How many different values

60
00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.900
can four bits count?

61
00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.900
Sixteen, from zero to fifteen:

62
00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.440
each extra bit doubles it.

63
00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.450
Remember: each bit is worth double

64
00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.170
the one on its right, eight bits

65
00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.890
make a byte, and more bits mean

66
00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.010
more detail, but more data.

67
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.400
Try it yourself: count to thirty-one on

68
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.120
one hand, with a friend checking.

69
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.040
Then write your age in binary.

70
00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.840
Next time, you'll meet the tiny

71
00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.880
computer that counts like this.
